<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>A carpenter in Kericho makes <m>x</m> chairs and <m>y</m> tables a week. Each of the chairs needs <m>4</m> metres of timber and each of the tables needs <m>7</m>; there are <m>44</m> metres of timber. Each of the chairs needs <m>9</m> hours of workshop time and each of the tables needs <m>2</m>; there are <m>33</m> hours of workshop time. The profit is Ksh <m>400</m> on each of the chairs and Ksh <m>500</m> on each of the tables.</p><p>The vertex where the two boundary lines meet is <m>{(2.6, 4.8)}</m>. It would give the greatest profit if fractions were allowed.</p><p>(a) Which whole-number plan gives the greatest profit? <m>x =</m> <fillin characters="4" name="ans1"/>  <m>y =</m> <fillin characters="4" name="ans2"/> </p><div/><p>(b) What is that profit? Ksh <fillin characters="8" name="ans3"/>  </p></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>A vertex that is not a whole-number point is not an answer: test the whole-number points of R around it. Rounding <m>{(2.6, 4.8)}</m> to <m>({3}, {5})</m> breaks a condition: <m>{4 \times 3 + 7 \times 5 = 47 \gt 44}</m>. Rounding a vertex can carry you outside R, so check every constraint.</p><p>Test the whole-number points near the vertex that satisfy both conditions and work out <m>P = {400}x + {500}y</m> at each. The best is <m>x = {2}</m>, <m>y = {5}</m>, with profit Ksh <m>{3300}</m>.</p></p></solution>
    <answer><p><m>2</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>5</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>3300</m></p></answer>
</stack-static>